# Ergonomic Workstation Improvement {hideTitle}

## Ergonomic Workstation Improvement

Poor workstation setup is one of the most common sources of musculoskeletal strain. An ergonomic assessment can identify corrections. The images below show the same workstation before and after a targeted ergonomic assessment.

Drag the slider to reveal the changes, or use the arrow keys if you prefer keyboard navigation.

::: comparison {layout=slider}
:: Before assessment
![Workstation before assessment, monitor below eye level, keyboard on desk surface with no wrist support, chair seat too low causing upward reach, cables scattered across the floor](/assets/workstation-before.jpg)

:: After assessment
![Workstation after assessment, monitor raised to eye level on an adjustable arm, keyboard tray at elbow height with a padded wrist rest, chair adjusted so feet rest flat, cables routed and secured](/assets/workstation-after.jpg)
:::

---

### What changed

The assessment addressed four risk factors in a single adjustment session:

| Risk factor | Before | After |
| Monitor height | Below eye level, causing neck flexion | Top of screen at eye level |
| Keyboard position | On desk surface, shoulders elevated | Keyboard tray at elbow height, shoulders relaxed |
| Seat height | Too low, arms reaching upward | Adjusted so feet flat, thighs parallel to floor |
| Cable management | Loose cables across floor (trip hazard) | Routed and secured with cable clips |

::: note {tone=success style=outline}
Ergonomic improvements do not require expensive equipment. Adjusting what is already at the workstation resolves most issues.
:::

---

### Reflection

::: free-response Identify one ergonomic risk at your own workstation or a workstation you observe regularly. {required level=3}
placeholder: Describe the risk factor (posture, reach distance, lighting, repetition), who is affected, and one practical change that could reduce or eliminate it. If a change is outside your authority, note who you would raise it with.
:::
